1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
You open your credit card app.

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:04,960
Last month you owed $5 ,000.

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:07,680
You made a $200 payment.

4
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:09,380
You didn't miss the deadline.

5
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:11,700
You did what you were supposed to do.

6
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,200
But now your balance is still around $4
,900.

7
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:17,000
Wait.

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:18,480
You paid $200.

9
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:22,000
Why did your debt only shrink by about
$100?

10
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:24,460
Where did the rest of your money go?

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,200
The answer is interest.

12
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:31,460
But the part that makes credit card debt
so frustrating is how interest

13
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:32,960
interacts with your payments,

14
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:35,280
your purchases, and time.

15
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:37,060
Let's make it visible.

16
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:42,920
Imagine you have a credit card with a $5
,000 balance and a

17
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:46,720
24 % annual percentage rate, or APR.

18
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:49,220
For a simple illustration,

19
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:53,700
24 % a year works out to about 2 % a
month.

20
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:58,240
2 % of $5 ,000 is $100.

21
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:03,240
That means carrying this balance for
roughly a month could add about

22
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:05,180
$100 in interest.

23
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:09,520
Actual credit cards often calculate
interest daily,

24
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,480
so your exact charge depends on things
like your balance throughout the

25
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:16,960
billing cycle and when your payment
arrives.

26
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:21,440
But this simplified example shows the
basic mechanics.

27
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:24,740
You start with $5 ,000.

28
00:01:25,860 --> 00:01:28,640
About $100 in interest gets added.

29
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:30,780
Then you pay $200.

30
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:34,740
Your new balance is about $4 ,900.

31
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:37,340
Your payment did two jobs.

32
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:42,560
It covered the interest that had
accumulated and it reduced the amount you

33
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:42,900
owed.

34
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:47,500
Only about half of that payment actually
moved you out of debt.

35
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:52,740
And that's why making a payment can feel
so different from making progress.

36
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:55,400
Now, here's something important.

37
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:59,320
Interest doesn't mean your balance can
never go down.

38
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:04,980
If you stop adding purchases and keep
making that same $200 payment,

39
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:06,580
your balance falls.

40
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:08,380
As it falls,

41
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:11,620
the interest charge generally falls too.

42
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:16,900
In our simplified example, 2 % of $4 ,900

43
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:18,380
is $98.

44
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:24,280
So the following month, a little more of
your payment reduces the debt.

45
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:27,300
At first, the difference looks tiny.

46
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:32,600
But over time, more of the same payment
goes toward reducing your balance

47
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:34,820
and less goes toward interest.

48
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:39,720
The problem is that a lot of people never
get to see this process work

49
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:40,240
uninterrupted.

50
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:43,540
Because life keeps happening.

51
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:45,160
You pay $200.

52
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:49,600
Then you put $70 of groceries on the card,

53
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:51,660
$30 of gas,

54
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:55,320
and a $20 subscription you forgot was
there.

55
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:58,760
That's $120 in new spending.

56
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:04,180
In our first month example, your payment
reduced the old balance by about

57
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:04,960
$100.

58
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:08,880
But you added $120 back.

59
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,360
Now you owe more than you started with.

60
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:16,020
You made a payment on time and your
balance still went up.

61
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:18,280
This is the treadmill effect.

62
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:23,220
Money leaves your checking account every
month, but your debt barely moves

63
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:27,720
because interest and new purchases keep
replacing what you paid off.

64
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:30,100
And then there's the minimum payment.

65
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:32,260
That number can look reassuring.

66
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:33,900
It's smaller.

67
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:35,700
It feels manageable.

68
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:41,260
And paying at least the minimum on time
generally keeps your account from

69
00:03:41,260 --> 00:03:42,400
being treated as late.

70
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:47,100
But the minimum payment is not a promise
that you'll pay off the card quickly.

71
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:50,620
The calculation varies by card issuer.

72
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:56,440
It might depend on a percentage of your
balance, interest and fees, or

73
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:57,880
a minimum dollar amount.

74
00:03:58,840 --> 00:03:59,780
And on many cards,

75
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:04,140
the required minimum gets smaller as the
balance gets smaller.

76
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:06,500
That sounds helpful.

77
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:11,280
But if you keep reducing your payment
along with it, you can stretch repayment

78
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:13,320
out for a very long time.

79
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:14,880
Think of it this way.

80
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:17,660
Imagine walking toward an exit.

81
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:20,500
At first, you take normal steps.

82
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:25,120
Then every time you get closer, you make
your next step a little smaller.

83
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,300
You're still moving forward.

84
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:30,300
But getting to the door takes much longer.

85
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:36,160
That's one reason a fixed monthly payment
can work differently from paying

86
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:38,820
whatever minimum appears on your
statement.

87
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:43,820
If you can afford to keep paying the same
amount as your balance falls,

88
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:46,860
more of that payment can go toward the
debt.

89
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:50,740
There's another detail that catches people
off -guard.

90
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:55,500
New purchases may start costing interest
sooner than they expect.

91
00:04:56,400 --> 00:05:00,480
Many credit cards offer a grace period on
purchases.

92
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:06,240
When you qualify for it, paying your full
statement balance by the due date

93
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:09,600
generally lets you avoid interest on those
purchases.

94
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:12,080
But if you carry a balance,

95
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:14,700
you may lose that grace period.

96
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:20,320
Depending on your card's terms, new
purchases can then begin accruing

97
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:22,440
interest from their transaction dates.

98
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:28,240
So using the same card for everyday
spending while you're trying to pay it off

99
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:30,760
can make the math even harder to outrun.

100
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:32,600
What actually helps?

101
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:38,820
First, separate paying down old debt from
paying for new expenses as much

102
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:40,400
as your situation allows.

103
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:43,960
If new purchases keep landing on the card,

104
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:48,480
it becomes much harder to tell whether
your repayment plan is working.

105
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:51,260
Second, look at the APR.

106
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:57,640
A $200 payment behaves very differently on
a card charging 12

107
00:05:57,640 --> 00:06:00,560
% than on one charging 30%.

108
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:02,460
The payment is the same.

109
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:05,360
The cost of carrying the balance isn’t.

110
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:10,800
Third, look for the minimum payment
warning on your credit card statement.

111
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:15,480
It shows how long repayment could take if
you make only minimum

112
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:18,840
payments, assuming no additional charges.

113
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:24,380
Your statement will also typically show a
payment amount that would pay off

114
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:26,580
the current balance in about three years.

115
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:32,240
Those numbers make the cost of a small
monthly payment much easier to see.

116
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:35,880
And finally, if you can afford to pay
extra,

117
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:38,820
understand what that extra money does.

118
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:42,040
In our simplified first month example,

119
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:47,060
a $200 payment reduced the balance by
about $100.

120
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:52,940
A $300 payment would reduce it by about
$200.

121
00:06:54,060 --> 00:06:56,940
The total payment is 50 % larger,

122
00:06:57,660 --> 00:07:01,100
but the reduction in debt is twice as
large that month.

123
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:05,940
And a lower balance means less interest
accumulating afterward.

124
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:11,000
Of course, an extra $100 isn't available
in

125
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,120
everyone's budget.

126
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:18,220
If the minimum itself is becoming
unaffordable, contacting your card

127
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:22,900
issuer early and asking about hardship
options can be a useful next

128
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:23,360
step.

129
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:27,400
The key is understanding what the numbers
are telling you.

130
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:30,240
Your payment is how much money you send.

131
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:34,160
Your progress is how much your balance
actually falls.

132
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:36,640
Those are two different numbers.

133
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:41,620
So the next time you open your credit card
app, don't just ask,

134
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:43,600
did I make my payment?

135
00:07:44,300 --> 00:07:46,400
Ask, how much less do I owe?

136
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:50,680
That's the number that tells you whether
you're getting closer to the exit.
